WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
Harlem Globetrotters.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.180
It's unpredictable.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:04.740
It's unequaled.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.360
It's an action-packed entertainment treat for kids of all ages.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:34.020
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.020
I'm Mike.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.260
And I'm Sean.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.160
Sean, you bet on the generals?

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:44.400
Sean, always. Got to bet on the underdog once in a while or you're never going to get enough action.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.060
Sean, we joke, but do you know about the infamous game where the generals actually won?

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.360
Sean, I'm going to be honest, Mike.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.240
I know so little about the Harlem Globetrotters.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.660
Sean, that's how little you know.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.100
You just proved how little you know about the Harlem Globetrotters.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.760
Sean, try and say Harlem Globetrotters five times fast, Mike.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.820
I don't even want to say it one time fast because we're going to have to say it a lot during this episode.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.980
I dunked on you a little bit there to use a basketball term, so I'll back off a little bit.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.780
Oh, is that where it comes from? Yeah, that's where it comes from, actually.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:26.120
Wow. Yeah, I know nothing about the Globetrotters aside from they play against the generals, so please enlighten me.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:36.360
Well, so I did go to a Harlem Globetrotters game when I was a kid, and so you have Talladega and I have the Harlem Globetrotters.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:42.320
Together, the two of us have just a—we are the experts and authority on these kinds of things.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.880
It's why people listen to us.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:50.680
I don't think anybody is going to listen to another opinion on NASCAR ever again after our episode last week.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.920
I'm pretty sure you're right.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:02:01.520
So the Harlem Globetrotters, you know, if you didn't know, I hate to say this, but it's all real.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.060
All of it is real.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.380
It actually happens.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.500
These two teams always have to play each other.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.620
And the Harlem Globetrotters are just a vastly superior team.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:12.580
Or maybe not.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.800
Maybe the generals are just having a bad century.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.020
But they are—but they're part of the same organization.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.640
They are. Well, yeah, if you look into it, I know, I know.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.340
If you look into the accounting, there's some weird stuff going on and everything.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.300
But it's—trust me, Sean, it's not scripted.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.360
It's all real.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:42.280
Those guys are putting their bodies on the line game after game to do these tricks in the—during the matches.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:52.340
Sure. And whatever kind of basketball kayfabe you need to pay—like, put forward, then by all means.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:03:03.720
It's just kind of crazy because the NBA has ignored the, like, 13,438-game winning streak that the Harlem Globetrotters are currently having.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.620
And they just won't let them into the league, nor will they let any of the players be drafted.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.740
It's kind of insane.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.400
It seems to be some anti-Harlem bias.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.680
Are there any basketball players from Harlem?

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.320
Like, in the NBA?

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.840
In the NBA? I don't—I—if you want to bet on something else crazy, you already bet on the generals.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.900
If you want to bet that there are no NBA players from Harlem, sure, I'll take that bet.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.940
But, uh, so here—here's another illustration of how little I knew about this.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:43.660
When I was a kid, obviously, when I was, you know, a teenager, I learned that the Harlem Globetrotters were a—an entertainment sports act.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.620
Um, but when I was younger, I thought that they were an NBA team.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.920
And they just, like, were historically great.

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.340
And that's why everybody, like, made references to them being amazing.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:03.240
Uh, it was kind of confusing to learn that there was, like, kind of a fake sport of it.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:04.940
Um, but—

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.080
I have no idea what you're talking about.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.240
Okay, how was the game?

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:15.600
The game that I went to was amazing because they were—I mean, you know, you've never seen dunks like these.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:22.320
The amount of time that they can spin a ball on their finger—I can't spin a ball on my finger for one second.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.540
So they are doing it an infinite amount more time than me.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.680
Well, in that case, like, most people do it infinite.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.060
Sure. I guess—yeah, I guess anybody's qualified to be on the Harlem Globetrotters.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:43.480
Um, and then what—what happened was the one time the generals won was because the Harlem Globetrotter team wasn't keeping track of the time.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.420
They knew they were down, and you're supposed to win, you know, in epic fashion.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.360
But, uh, they weren't keeping track of the time, and they were down 12 with two point—down 12 with two minutes left to go.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.300
And they could not make it up.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.340
And, uh, they lost.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.980
And apparently there was some—there was some tears, uh, from the fans.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.300
There was, um, there was probably an investigation in Las Vegas.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.160
If anybody bet on the generals that game, there was probably an investigation.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.780
Um, but I think it's just funny that, uh, that the generals did happen to win just that one time.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:24.760
And all the more reason why it is so strange, uh, that you can play as the generals in this game.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.120
Um, it is the Harlem Globetrotters versus the generals.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:29.240
That's the league.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:36.420
You can do four-player NES action here, where two players can be on the Harlem Globetrotters,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:37.680
and two players can be on the generals.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:44.460
Why would you ever pick the Washington generals to be the team of your choice?

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.000
I cannot understand.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.080
Because you like underdogs, obviously.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.640
Yeah, but they don't even get to do any of the cool tricks in the game.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:04.760
Like, honestly, like—so, okay, when I opened up the manual, um, I thought that I was getting a very different game than what this turned out to be.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:15.180
Like, I saw a control scheme that said that you, as playing as the Globetrotters, could also sort of control the generals.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:23.380
So that it almost turned it into this very, like, like, you know, sports entertainment experience.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:30.260
Where you were just trying to find ways to make the most, like, weird plays happen.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.540
Because you're kind of controlling both teams at once.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.640
And that is not at all what's there.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.260
Like, this is a game in which there is vast asymmetry.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:50.760
Where if you play as the Globetrotters, you get to play regular basketball, but with trick shot and trick pass, uh, availability.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.960
And then if you're the generals, you just don't get to do that.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.440
Um. Playing on the generals is just hard mode.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:05.700
Yes. I've never seen something like this, where there's play with cool features or play with nothing.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:06.520
Um.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.040
But this game does it.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.120
It does it.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.740
It doesn't let you do it right away, though.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.540
You're greeted with a pretty antagonistic, um, starting screen here.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.700
Uh. Did you have any issues starting up a game?

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:19.780
Do you know what I'm talking about?

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.020
Um. I kept pressing, like, A, B, start.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.420
And I kept, like, cycling.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.560
Is that what you mean?

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.200
Yes. Okay. Because what happens is, so first there are two splash screens.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.620
One for Game Tech Presents and then one for all the credits in the game.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:36.980
You can't do anything.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.280
You just have to watch those as if this is some kind of, like, PS1 game with loading or something.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:46.160
And then it starts, it goes right into what appears to be the game.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:50.520
Only it's doing, like, a, you know, tech demo of the game.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.080
So it says, press start to begin.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.320
So you press start.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:59.960
So now you're treated with this, like, idea that start is the way to confirm things in this game.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.300
You immediately go to the questions.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.600
The Harlem Globetrotters are asking you questions about the kind of game you want to play.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.380
And, like, you know, what's going on here?

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.920
Have you seen the player?

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.060
He's got, like, he's got a really scary smile.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.420
He's got, like, a Joker smile going on.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.580
I mean, I've seen the player.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:28.580
Yes, he does. His face does contort in unnatural ways that are quite uncanny.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.420
And is there a big shadowy guy behind him?

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:32.400
Like, what's going on there?

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.160
There's just this big black outline.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.480
You don't see outlines around people like that?

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:37.520
Not like that.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.860
What do you mean people like that, Sean?

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:46.060
Whoa. No, it's like you're just obviously not one of the people that see auras.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:49.040
Of course. I can see your aura, Mike.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.000
That's what they mean.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.820
The Harlem Globetrotters are aura farming.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.520
Now I'm hip with the kids.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.380
Like, old-timey aura.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:57.680
Oh, right, right.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.760
That girl in college that had all the crystals kinds of auras.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:07.640
Yeah. The Harlem Globetrotters do have a bit of an old-timey quality to them.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.480
Even that, like, their song, their theme song, which sounds crazy, right?

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.660
Whistle it. Well, I can't whistle it because I can't whistle.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.800
Do it right now. No, see, I can't do it.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.320
I miss, like, one note and I'm, like, complete.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.460
I start doing a different song.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.120
But the funny thing is, is, like, how many other, like, do the Buffalo Bills have a theme song?

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.260
Yes. No, no, no.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:34.280
So, like, forever, like, one that, like, like, a theme song that they come out to?

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.360
To come out to?

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.000
I mean, no. But.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.440
You know what I mean? Like, like, like, wrestling has, like, The Rock has his theme song.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.320
You know, Roman Reigns has his theme song.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.940
The Sabres have a theme song. Okay, so, so there, it is, like, a thing to have a theme song that you come out to.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.680
Yes. Got it.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.400
Not in my sports teams.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.220
You have that Italian song on the seventh inning stretch?

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.480
Oh, yeah, yeah, sure. There's traditions, right?

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.820
Like, we even have Meet the Mets, but, like, we're not playing that before every game as

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:07.240
if it's, like, oh, my God, that's the New York Mets music.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:08.540
Like, they're showing up as a surprise.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:09.760
You are playing that every game.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.500
Right. But the Harlem Globetrotters, on the other hand, you know, they do have this, that song

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.120
with the whistle that is kind of, it has, like, a barbershop quality to it, you know?

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:25.360
Yes. I, again, I've never been to a game, so I don't know what the vibe is supposed to be, but

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:32.460
I always imagined it being, like, this is the most wholesome possible product you could

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:40.040
ever see. And that whistle song is probably the most, like, the biggest thing that informs that

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:42.620
uninformed opinion.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.680
You know, I didn't want to say anything, but now that I'm looking at it, the Harlem Globetrotters

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.360
are very barbershop coded.

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:50.680
Barbershop coded.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:56.400
Yeah, because, like, think about it, the basketball that they use is not the same as the NBA with

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.800
the orange. It is a red, white, and blue ball to match their red, white, and blue jerseys and everything.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.020
To match their barber pole spinning thing?

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:10.860
Right, exactly. When they spin that ball, right, the red, white, and blue, it matches that barber pole.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.100
Most of them have haircuts.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.460
Exactly. You have to open your eyes.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.080
Dude, you're getting it, you know?

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.140
I think that this whole thing was by Big Barber to get you to go get a haircut.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.340
A buzz cut, even, you know?

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.260
Just, like, why screw it, you know?

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.460
It's like, shave all your hair off, and then, like, we'll keep doing that for you.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:34.920
But buzz cut people statistically have to go to the barber more often than people who let

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:35.720
their hair grow out.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:41.220
Well, I mean, they probably don't want everybody to be buzz cut people because they might catch

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.100
on that they can just do that at home.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.860
They want at least some people with, like, regular haircuts.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.760
Thankfully, the theme song for the Harlem Globetrotters is included in the game.

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.940
It is one of two tracks, but don't worry, it's in there.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:00.840
Just like we were worried about if the Simpsons theme song would be included, the Harlem Globetrotters

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.260
theme song was included as well.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:05.300
That and the Harlem Shake.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:07.720
Sure, yeah. Do the Harlem Shake.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:14.940
And that just reminded me that I completely lost my point about this whole section, which

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:19.760
was the antagonistic menu of, remember, folks, rewind a little bit.

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.460
The button that you press to start the game officially is the start button.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:29.720
Then the Harlem Globetrotters ask you a bunch of questions of which you must press select

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.900
to lock in your choice.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:38.640
Not start. You must press select because you are, I guess, selecting that option.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:44.240
It goes against the Nintendo logic where select was to, you know, kind of switch between modes.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:53.420
But here we are. And then as you're at the end, it then says press select to begin Harlem Globetrotters basketball,

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.340
which is interesting because you could have picked the generals and it still says press

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.740
select to begin Harlem Globetrotters basketball.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:06.540
But still, press select to begin after just seeing press start to begin, not a few screens

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.240
earlier, is annoying.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.480
It's just downright confusing design.

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:17.100
Well, I guess maybe they're thinking it more philosophically, like to press start to begin

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:25.540
is something of a tautology, you know, like, like push start to begin, like press go to

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:29.680
continue. Like that's, that's too, it's repetitive.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.580
You got to switch it up sometimes.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:33.820
Sure. Yeah. Keep people on their toes.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:14:10.460
Mm hmm. All right.

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.380
I don't know.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:45.580
Still to just like basically become five on five basketball is like, what are we doing

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:46.940
here? Yeah.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:54.340
It's pretty weird that I'll get into some things that I think they did right later.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:15:00.640
But in terms of presentation, like you'd think that double dribble was the Globetrotters

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:05.320
game because it had those sort of cut scene cut ins.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:13.320
Yeah. When you do, when you, when you do the dunks or just a good shot in general, like, and that

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.080
there's like none of that flair here.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:17.940
Like there is some flair.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:25.580
Um, but it's kind of happening in miniature in front of you where when you think about

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:31.420
how larger than life, uh, that the Globetrotters, I mean, I guess you're the one that would

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:36.720
be able to say this, um, are and how wild it is.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:38.020
It's just not there. Yeah.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.220
They have like superpowers.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:43.600
You know what I mean? Like they have the ability to like, all of a sudden one of them is on like stilts, you

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:47.460
know? And then they're like, just like, they're just right next to the basket and they just like

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:48.300
get it passed to them.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:49.900
And that's like their version of an alley-oop.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:54.820
It's like, they have, they bend the rules of basketball to their liking to do, you know,

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:56.380
fun stunts and everything.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:16:01.380
And they'll take shots from like half court and they will miraculously go in or they'll

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:06.240
do like really fancy dribbling, you know, just to like show off that they can do this

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.860
while they're versing another team.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:10.080
And none of that really happens here.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:15.640
The closest to like a comedic moment we get is when the ref trips on the way to the foul

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:16.980
line. Did you see that happening?

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:17.960
Yeah, that's pretty fun.

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:19.020
That happens every time.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.280
I feel like it does, but it's, but it was unexpected the first time.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.280
I was like, oh, that actually got a good laugh out of me.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:32.240
Yeah. Um, the other sort of larger than life moments are like, you know, the, the trick pass and

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:33.620
the trick shots.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:41.300
And there's a couple, I feel like they, they changed contextually depending on where on the

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:42.200
court you are.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:51.160
Um, the most like unbelievable one is a dunk that the dude does like five or six flips in

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:57.020
midair. Um, but I did not know how to trigger that again, after I saw it randomly, the rest of

00:16:57.020 --> 00:17:03.340
